\section{Introduction}
The study of the molecular structure of the DNA/RNA molecule has been central to biochemistry since the search for the double helix. Understanding the geometry of the nucleotides allows us to predict their base pairing properties, which in turn dictates the function of the cell. This investigation focuses on the structural relationships between the strands, the recognition sites for specific bases, and the thermodynamic stability of the double helix.

\section{The Strands}
The DNA double helix consists of two strands of nucleotides, held together by hydrogen bonds between the phosphate groups of the purines and the deoxyribose sugar. The sugar is deoxyribose (C12H22O11), and the backbone is formed by alternating phosphate groups with sugar rings. The nitrogenous bases are adenine (A), thymine (T), cytosine (C), and guanine (G).

\section{Base Pairs and Recognition Sites}
The A-T and G-C base pairs form hydrogen bonds that span the helix. These bonds create a stable base stack where the nitrogenous bases hydrogen bond to the phosphate groups of the next strand. The recognition sites are specific locations along the helical axis where complementary base pairs align. There are 4 compatible pairs: G-C, A-T, C-G, and T-A. Only two of these pairs are visible in the double helix because they form hydrogen bonds with each other.

\section{Geometry and Stranding}
The DNA molecule is described by the Watson-Crick model. The strands are antiparallel, meaning they run in opposite directions. The central axis runs through the middle of both strands. The nucleobase on the right-hand strand is always the sugar (deoxyribose), and the base on the left-hand strand is always the phosphate. This configuration ensures that the sugar-phosphate backbone is perpendicular to the axis.
